trigonometry running on Cloudflare Workers/Pages with zero third-party latency."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"p-5 rounded-2xl bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60","children":[["$","svg",null,{"ref":"$undefined","xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-shield-check w-6 h-6 text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 mb-2","children":[["$","path","oel41y",{"d":"M20 13c0 5-3.5 7.5-7.66 8.95a1 1 0 0 1-.67-.01C7.5 20.5 4 18 4 13V6a1 1 0 0 1 1-1c2 0 4.5-1.2 6.24-2.72a1.17 1.17 0 0 1 1.52 0C14.51 3.81 17 5 19 5a1 1 0 0 1 1 1z"}],["$","path","dzmm74",{"d":"m9 12 2 2 4-4"}],"$undefined"]}],["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm","children":"Scholarly Verification"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-1 leading-relaxed","children":"Reviewed by graduates of classical Islamic seminaries and verified against authoritative observatory sightings."}]]}]]}]
c:["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 rounded-3xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 p-6 sm:p-10 shadow-xs space-y-6 transition-colors duration-200","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100","children":"The Heritage of Islamic Astronomy and Mawaqit Science"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed","children":["The determination of prayer times is historically known in Islamic civilization as ",["$","em",null,{"children":"Ilm al-Mawaqit"}]," (the science of sacred timekeeping). While ancient civilizations tracked seasons for agriculture and governance, the advent of Islam elevated celestial observation to a sacred duty. The Quran repeatedly directs humanity to contemplate the cosmic order:"]}],["$","blockquote",null,{"className":"p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border-l-4 border-emerald-600 dark:border-emerald-500 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-sm italic","children":"“Indeed, in the creation of the heavens and the earth and the alternation of the night and the day are signs for those of understanding.” — Surah Ali ‘Imran (3:190)"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed","children":"During the golden age of Islamic scholarship, legendary polymaths such as Al-Battani (Albatenius), Ibn al-Haytham (Alhazen), Al-Biruni, and the 14th-century timekeeper of the Umayyad Mosque in Damascus, Shams al-Din Ibn al-Shatir, revolutionized the science of the astrolabe (*al-Asturlab*) and the celestial quadrant. These scholars derived exact mathematical models to determine the solar zenith, the entry of afternoon twilight, and the shadow lengths corresponding to Asr prayer across diverse geographic latitudes."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed","children":"At Namaz-e-Shariq, we honor this noble tradition by implementing modern, high-precision astronomical algorithms that honor the observations of our intellectual ancestors while adapting to the needs of modern metropolitan life."}]]}]
d:["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 rounded-3xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 p-6 sm:p-10 shadow-xs space-y-6 transition-colors duration-200","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100","children":"Mathematical Solar Coordinate Methodology"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed","children":"Our proprietary calculation engine tracks real-time solar declination and the Equation of Time (EoT) based on the continuous Julian Day count. The specific parameters governing our five daily waqt intervals are outlined below:"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-4 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"p-4 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700/60","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-base mb-1","children":"1. Subh Sadiq (Fajr Dawn Twilight)"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed","children":"Fajr begins when the upper edge of the sun reaches a specified depression angle below the true eastern horizon. In accordance with the extensive optical field expeditions conducted by the University of Islamic Sciences Banuri Town, Karachi, and the Muslim World League, we adopt the 18.0° depression angle for South Asia and continental regions. For the Arabian Peninsula, our system dynamically switches to the 18.5° Umm Al-Qura standard."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"p-4 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700/60","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-base mb-1","children":"2. Shurooq (Sunrise) and Expiration of Fajr"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed","children":"Sunrise is defined as the precise moment the upper limb of the solar disk breaches the geometric horizon, factoring in standard atmospheric refraction (34 arcminutes) and the solar semidiameter (16 arcminutes), yielding an effective zenith angle of 90° 50’ (0.833°). Once sunrise begins, all prayer is strictly prohibited (*Makruh Tahrimi*) until the sun ascends the height of a spear."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"p-4 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700/60","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-base mb-1","children":"3. Zawal and Dhuhr (Midday Prayer)"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed","children":"Solar noon (*Nisf al-Nahar*) occurs when the sun crosses the local celestial meridian. To avoid the forbidden instant of exact solar zenith, our engine adds a mandatory safety precaution margin (*Ihtiyat*) of 2 to 3 minutes, ensuring that Dhuhr begins only after the sun has indisputably commenced its descent westward."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"p-4 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700/60","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-base mb-1","children":"4. Asr: The Dual Juristic Shadow Standard"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed","children":"Our platform provides full support for both classical interpretations of Asr time:"}],["$","ul",null,{"className":"list-disc pl-5 mt-2 space-y-1 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400","children":[["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Shafi’i, Maliki, Hanbali (Factor 1):"}]," Shadow of an object equals its own length plus the baseline shadow cast at solar noon."]}],"$L19"]}]]}],"$L1a"]}]]}]
e:["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 rounded-3xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 p-6 sm:p-10 shadow-xs space-y-6 transition-colors duration-200","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100","children":"Editorial Integrity, Scholarly Review & Independence"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed","children":"The content published across Namaz-e-Shariq adheres to strict scholarly standards:"}],["$","ul",null,{"className":"list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed","children":[["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Primary Source Verification:"}]," All fiqh guides, rakat breakdowns, and rulings on prayer invalidation cite classical recognized manuals, including *Al-Hidayah*, *Radd al-Muhtar (Fatawa Shami)*, *Al-Majmu’ Sharh al-Muhadhdhab*, and *Al-Mughni*."]}],["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Multi-Madhab Inclusivity:"}]," Rather than imposing a single regional custom, we transparently delineate differences between Sunni schools of jurisprudence with academic fairness and deep respect for legitimate scholarly differences (*Ikhtilaf*)."]}],["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Non-Commercial Religious Service:"}]," Our core prayer calculation tools and educational articles are completely free from obstructive paywalls, pop-up advertisements, and commercial data harvesting."]}],["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Continuous Auditing:"}]," Local masajid and verified users can communicate discrepancies through our dedicated ",["$","$L1b",null,{"href":"/contact","className":"text-brand-700 dark:text-emerald-400 font-bold hover:underline","children":"Contact Desk"}],", where reports are reviewed by our celestial astronomy researchers and certified scholars."]}]]}]]}]
f:["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 rounded-3xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 p-6 sm:p-10 shadow-xs space-y-6 transition-colors duration-200","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100","children":"The Spiritual Psychology and Circadian Harmony of Salah"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed","children":"Beyond mathematical formulas and legal categorizations, Salah serves as an essential spiritual therapy for the human psyche. Modern chronobiology demonstrates that the human body operates on circadian cycles tightly synchronized with solar illumination. By pausing five times a day at major solar transitions, the Muslim worshiper achieves regular intervals of mindfulness, neurological relaxation, and spiritual realignment:"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"p-4 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700/60","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 block mb-1","children":"Morning Renewal (Fajr)"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed","children":"Awakening before sunrise activates morning cortisol and endorphin release in a state of quiet meditation, providing mental focus and spiritual fortification before worldly engagements commence."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"p-4 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700/60","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 block mb-1","children":"Midday Decompression (Dhuhr)"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed","children":"At the peak of workplace activity, Dhuhr interrupts cognitive fatigue, encouraging physical ablution (Wudu) which cools the nervous system and restores emotional calm."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"p-4 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700/60","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 block mb-1","children":"Afternoon Perspective (Asr)"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed","children":"As the shadows lengthen and physical energy wanes, Asr redirects the heart toward gratitude, reminding the soul of the fleeting nature of time and worldly enterprise."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"p-4 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700/60","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 block mb-1","children":"Evening Peace (Maghrib & Isha)"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed","children":"Marking the boundary between labor and familial rest, evening prayers facilitate transition into restful nocturnal contemplation, preparing the body for wholesome sleep."}]]}]]}]]}]
19:["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Hanafi (Factor 2):"}]," Shadow of an object equals twice its length plus the baseline midday shadow, occurring approximately 45 to 60 minutes later."]}]
1a:["$","div",null,{"className":"p-4 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700/60","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-base mb-1","children":"5. Maghrib and Isha (Evening Twilight)"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed","children":"Maghrib commences instantaneously when the solar disk disappears completely below the western horizon. Fasting believers break their fast at this moment. Isha commences when the remaining evening twilight (*al-Shafaq*) has vanished from the western horizon, calculated using an 18.0° depression angle (*Shafaq Abyad*) or a 90-minute interval according to regional conventions."}]]}]
